1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,280
Choosing the right SaaS idea can be the difference between making $10,000, $100,000, or even

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:08,320
a million dollars a month and making nothing at all.

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:11,960
But I'm not here to give you another list of hypothetical SaaS ideas, because that's

4
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:13,240
not what you need.

5
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:18,200
What you really need is to know how successful founders actually found their idea and how

6
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:22,240
you can steal their tactics to find your own idea today.

7
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:23,240
So here's what I did.

8
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:28,280
I surveyed over 200 SaaS founders, making at least $1,000 a month, some making well

9
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:34,200
over $100,000 a month to uncover the exact methods they used to find their winning ideas.

10
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,940
This is something no one else on YouTube is doing.

11
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:41,680
People are talking out of their opinion and limited experience, but on this channel, I

12
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:45,640
like to get some data and provide you with some insights that you can't get anywhere

13
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:46,640
else.

14
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:50,160
So in this video, I'm going to break down the top eight ways these founders discovered

15
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:54,080
profitable SaaS opportunities, and I'll give you examples for each.

16
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:58,840
I've seen these methods work in my own companies and across the hundreds of startups I'm invested

17
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:04,040
in, as well as the thousands of SaaS companies that I've seen started in the broader tiny

18
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:07,480
seed microconf and startups for the rest of us ecosystem.

19
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:10,440
There's a lot to cover in this video, so let's dive in.

20
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:15,680
Method number one was used by a shocking 48% of founders that we surveyed.

21
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:18,120
And honestly, didn't surprise me at all.

22
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:23,220
For years, I've said, don't tell me your idea, tell me what problem it solves.

23
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:27,820
And for almost half of the successful founders we surveyed, that problem showed up right

24
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:30,960
where they were already spending 40 hours a week.

25
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:34,340
Method number one is finding a problem at your day job.

26
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:38,220
So this is where you spot a problem while working your regular job, either as a developer

27
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:43,720
frustrated by bad tools or as a subject matter expert who knows your industry's pain points.

28
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:48,260
You already have context, you have credibility and access to potential customers.

29
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:53,100
One example of this is Alex Yumashev, the founder of JITbit, which is a help desk ticketing

30
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:58,260
Alex told us, while working my full-time job, I realized that many existing tech support

31
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:03,460
and customer support apps were missing the boring features that real teams actually need.

32
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:09,300
Things like SSO, active directory integration, user provisioning, on-prem, self-hosted deployment

33
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:11,980
for Windows, and many other features.

34
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:14,380
The sleek modern tools had none of that.

35
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:18,540
And the ones that did have the boring features look like they were straight out of the 90s.

36
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:23,660
Buttered UIs, endless check boxes, painfully complicated setup flows.

37
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:27,500
And while the market was very saturated, I took that as a good sign.

38
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:29,260
The market was there.

39
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:33,880
Attacking a big competitor can be a benefit on one hand, stealing cool marketing ideas

40
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:36,140
from the smaller companies on the other.

41
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:39,800
Maybe you've spent years working in an industry and you're a subject matter expert.

42
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:44,140
You know all the headaches inside and out, like Zach from Upstream Edge.

43
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:50,300
Zach told us, I provide a desktop application for forecasting and valuing oil and gas wells

44
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:54,180
that include sophisticated engineering and financial analysis tools.

45
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:59,180
100 times faster speed and higher level features compared to alternatives are what differentiate

46
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:00,180
my product.

47
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:04,700
I worked in the oil and gas industry as a petroleum engineer for a decade, and my frustration

48
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:10,260
with the current tools led me to learn how to build new tools to solve our daily problems.

49
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:14,660
I had a burning desire to see better software, and I decided to make the product I thought

50
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:15,900
the industry needed.

51
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:18,860
Then there's Lee Dido, the founder of Didomite.

52
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:23,220
Lee told us, it's the fastest way to get looping video on a public screen.

53
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:28,460
I started working at a company called BrightSign in 2012, who's the market leader in digital

54
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:30,380
signage media players.

55
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:34,540
It's a very hardware-focused industry, and I repeatedly kept seeing the same problems

56
00:03:34,540 --> 00:03:37,900
over and over when it came to operational software.

57
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:41,860
Because nobody was thinking about user experience, and I kept socking away my ideas until one

58
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:44,900
day, 10 years later, I thought it was time to jump.

59
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:47,260
Consider jobs you've had, even a long time ago.

60
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:51,300
The problem I just mentioned was something Lee experienced a decade earlier.

61
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:56,140
Think about those tedious, repetitive tasks that are currently being human-automated,

62
00:03:56,140 --> 00:03:58,300
and that could be turned into software.

63
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:02,660
The next example is similar in that it's automating a task that this consultant did

64
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:03,660
for their clients.

65
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:09,140
Ahmed Babakir is the co-founder of Blue Gamma, which is interest rate forecasts for finance

66
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:10,140
teams.

67
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:14,260
Ahmed told us, my co-founder and I used to work at a consultancy where clients would

68
00:04:14,260 --> 00:04:18,540
ask us for interest rate data, sometimes multiple times a day.

69
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:23,820
As a recent graduate, my job was to pull the data, format it, and send it over in a spreadsheet

70
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:24,820
every time.

71
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:28,900
After leaving the company and learning about startups a year later, we realized it was

72
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:32,260
a repetitive problem that software could solve very easily.

73
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:36,060
Or maybe at your day job, you see a colleague doing repetitive work that you know could

74
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:40,380
be streamlined, like Steve from LUTI, formerly Metro Retro.

75
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:44,980
LUTI is collaboration software focused on development teams, helping them with retrospectives,

76
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:47,140
refinement, planning, and so on.

77
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:52,020
Steve told us, I created an early prototype of a canvas-based retro tool as part of a

78
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:53,940
hackathon at my last real job.

79
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:58,380
The idea came from watching the PM write up the notes after every meeting by hand.

80
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:03,020
So now let's take a step back and look at what makes this first method of finding a

81
00:05:03,020 --> 00:05:04,860
problem at your day job work.

82
00:05:04,860 --> 00:05:08,340
The first is, you already have deep context around the problem.

83
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:09,340
You've lived it.

84
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:10,500
You know the workarounds.

85
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:13,600
You understand why existing solutions fall short.

86
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:17,740
The second is you have built-in access to potential customers and beta testers.

87
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:21,020
These are your colleagues, your industry contacts, and so on.

88
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:23,940
The third is that you understand the buying process.

89
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:28,900
You know who signs off on purchases, what budget cycles look like, what objections come

90
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:29,900
up.

91
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:33,980
And the fourth is that you can potentially validate the idea while still employed, reducing

92
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:34,980
your risk.

93
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:37,540
But there are three things to watch out for with this approach.

94
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:42,100
The first is make sure the problem exists beyond your specific company.

95
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:45,080
Your employer might just be uniquely dysfunctional.

96
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:48,260
So validate your idea outside of your little bubble.

97
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:51,580
The second is check your employment agreement for IP clauses.

98
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:55,180
Some companies claim ownership of anything you build while you're employed.

99
00:05:55,180 --> 00:06:00,340
And the third is be careful not to use proprietary information or customer data from your employer

100
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:01,660
to build your product.

101
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:05,700
I also wouldn't use any of my employer's hardware like a laptop.

102
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:07,980
I would have a personal laptop that I worked on.

103
00:06:07,980 --> 00:06:11,840
And as I mentioned, I would check that IP clause and be really clean about it.

104
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:17,060
So with this method, I'd be asking myself three questions to try to get my juices flowing.

105
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:22,600
The first is what tools do I use on a daily basis that frustrate me or my coworkers?

106
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:28,560
The second is what manual process or processes exist today that I think should be automated

107
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:29,640
by software?

108
00:06:29,640 --> 00:06:35,100
And the third is, are the incumbent tools in this industry actually old and clunky and

109
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:36,220
not changing?

110
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:39,220
Or are they actually pretty good and I just don't like them?

111
00:06:39,220 --> 00:06:42,300
So there were a lot of companies that used this first method.

112
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:44,240
As I said, it was almost 50%.

113
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:46,580
But I don't want to spend this whole video talking about it.

114
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:52,360
So I put together a completely free resource over at robwalling.com slash ideas.

115
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:53,780
I'll tell you more about that later.

116
00:06:53,780 --> 00:06:57,580
But one thing I want you to notice is that all of these folks were solving problems that

117
00:06:57,580 --> 00:06:59,740
people would be willing to pay for.

118
00:06:59,740 --> 00:07:03,860
In the next category, the founders knew that there would be a customer ready to pay because

119
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:07,320
they copied an existing idea or an existing product.

120
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:10,040
This was about 10% of our respondents.

121
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:14,420
In some cases, founders saw how much their employer was spending on a solution and thought

122
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:16,980
I could do that cheaper or I could do that better.

123
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:20,440
Or you might notice a large space with a hated competitor.

124
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:25,020
While I didn't participate in this particular survey, I did exactly this when I was building

125
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:26,020
Drip.

126
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:29,700
And we did it by accident, if I'm being honest, we had built a little email capture widget

127
00:07:29,700 --> 00:07:34,120
and the ability to send a sequences of emails to people that signed up.

128
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:36,780
What we quickly realized is there was market pull.

129
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:41,800
There were people talking about marketing automation and email automation features that

130
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:45,580
didn't exist in the less expensive email service providers.

131
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:50,600
And every tool that had these features was really expensive, had annual contracts, software

132
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:54,820
wasn't great, and they required you to talk to a salesperson.

133
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:57,280
So we started building some of those features.

134
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:02,280
And while we didn't copy any existing products, we did enter an existing category.

135
00:08:02,280 --> 00:08:07,040
And there were already paradigms in place that we certainly slotted into, and it made

136
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:10,140
selling and marketing our tool a lot easier.

137
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:15,580
Because when people arrived at the homepage, drip.com, they could see, Oh, this is this

138
00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:16,740
type of tool.

139
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:17,800
Why is it different?

140
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:18,960
And that's all I had to communicate.

141
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,800
I didn't have to communicate what we were doing and how novel we were.

142
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:26,160
I just had to say, we are either an email service provider or at some point we were

143
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:30,960
in email automation software and then we became lightweight marketing automation.

144
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:36,700
And then I just had to tell you why are we better or different than all the other incumbents.

145
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:39,860
And there were a lot of big clunky incumbents that people didn't like.

146
00:08:39,860 --> 00:08:43,780
And that in addition to the fact that we executed really well, was the reason that drip grew

147
00:08:43,780 --> 00:08:48,860
so quickly and resulted in a life-changing exit for me back in 2016.

148
00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:53,980
Another example of using this approach comes from Tim Leland, the founder of t.ly.

149
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:58,940
t.ly is an all-in-one URL shortener and QR code platform.

150
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:02,320
It's built for marketers, developers, creators, and teams.

151
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:06,420
And Tim told us, I was working for a company on an online giving product, and we were

152
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:09,360
tasked to build a text to give feature.

153
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:15,320
To do this, we had to shorten the URLs since SMS messages are limited to 160 characters.

154
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:19,660
We looked at Bitly and saw it was thousands of dollars to use a custom domain to create

155
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:20,660
short links.

156
00:09:20,660 --> 00:09:24,940
So to save money, I built a simple in-house URL shortener over the weekend that we could

157
00:09:24,940 --> 00:09:26,140
host ourselves.

158
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:29,980
This got me thinking other companies may want a more affordable option, but they don't want

159
00:09:29,980 --> 00:09:31,700
to build it in-house.

160
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:34,620
Another example of a founder who used this approach is Lillia Tobin.

161
00:09:34,620 --> 00:09:38,900
She started Big Mailer, which is an email marketing platform with a focus on multi-brand

162
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:41,500
management for agencies and franchises.

163
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:46,220
Lillia told us, while working on our previous B2C startup, we had grown our email list to

164
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:48,840
more than 300,000 subscribers.

165
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:52,700
All the available email solutions seemed too expensive for a product update newsletter

166
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:57,020
of our size, so we built an in-house solution powered by Amazon SES.

167
00:09:57,020 --> 00:10:00,300
Then we thought others might have a similar need and decided to make it into a standalone

168
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:01,300
tool.

169
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:07,500
The option might be to port an existing product to a new country, language, or geography.

170
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:11,580
We had a couple respondents who copied an idea that seemed to be working in the U.S.,

171
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:15,420
and they translated it to the needs of different geographies.

172
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:19,960
One example is Checkout Joy, which provides custom checkout pages for e-learning businesses

173
00:10:19,960 --> 00:10:23,380
that connect local payment processors with LMS platforms.

174
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:25,620
The founder, Mering DeWett, told us,

175
00:10:25,620 --> 00:10:30,540
My wife had a membership business and couldn't easily accept payments from their LMS platform.

176
00:10:30,540 --> 00:10:34,080
No stripe where we live and very limited PayPal usage.

177
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:37,180
Locals don't want to pay in USD, but that was all the LMS offered.

178
00:10:37,180 --> 00:10:41,000
I was researching solutions and realized that there are potentially thousands of businesses

179
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,500
with the same issue, so I built a quick MVP with our local payment processor and got them

180
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:46,660
live within a week.

181
00:10:46,660 --> 00:10:50,740
We could see an immediate bump in conversions for them, and it made it easy to do business

182
00:10:50,740 --> 00:10:53,700
and quickly get funds into their local bank account.

183
00:10:53,700 --> 00:10:58,420
Another founder who took this approach is Simon Thompson, the founder of Hooroo.ai,

184
00:10:58,420 --> 00:11:02,860
which is an AI answering service to help local Australian business owners manage phone

185
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:05,780
calls, especially during weekends and after hours.

186
00:11:05,780 --> 00:11:08,940
It has local Australian voices and sensibilities.

187
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:09,940
Simon told us,

188
00:11:09,940 --> 00:11:14,140
I saw examples in the U.S., such as Rosy AI, doing the same thing, and figured there would

189
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:19,740
be a need for the same type of service, but specifically with Australian accents and sensibilities.

190
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:22,740
I also guessed that it wouldn't make sense for the major players in the U.S. to build

191
00:11:22,740 --> 00:11:26,820
Australian accents into their platform and go to market in Australia, at least not any

192
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:28,300
time in the near future.

193
00:11:28,340 --> 00:11:30,300
So what makes this method work?

194
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:31,500
Well, four things.

195
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:33,940
The first is you have proof of demand.

196
00:11:33,940 --> 00:11:36,500
Someone else already validated that people will pay for it.

197
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:40,780
The second is you can learn from the incumbent's mistakes by reading their negative reviews

198
00:11:40,780 --> 00:11:41,780
and support forms.

199
00:11:41,780 --> 00:11:45,700
You can see what they're doing right and see what they're doing wrong because they pioneered

200
00:11:45,700 --> 00:11:46,700
it.

201
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:51,500
The third is you can differentiate on price, user experience, specific features, or a particular

202
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:52,500
niche.

203
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:55,460
And if the incumbent has been around for a long time, they've often raised prices and

204
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:58,540
gone up market, and that can leave a lot of opportunity.

205
00:11:58,540 --> 00:12:02,820
The fourth is if the market is large enough, even a small slice can be a great business

206
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:04,740
if you're a mostly bootstrap founder.

207
00:12:04,740 --> 00:12:08,260
But there are also four things to watch out for when using this approach.

208
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:12,780
The first is copying a product in a small market means you're fighting for scraps.

209
00:12:12,780 --> 00:12:15,620
This approach works best when the market is big.

210
00:12:15,620 --> 00:12:18,860
The second is make sure you have a real differentiator.

211
00:12:18,860 --> 00:12:20,460
It's not just we're newer.

212
00:12:20,460 --> 00:12:21,460
That's not enough.

213
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:25,420
But when we use the word copy, what we really mean is enter an existing category where

214
00:12:25,420 --> 00:12:26,860
there are already other players.

215
00:12:26,860 --> 00:12:31,220
I still think you need to get in and position yourself in a corner of the market that isn't

216
00:12:31,220 --> 00:12:32,660
being served very well.

217
00:12:32,660 --> 00:12:37,260
The third thing to watch out for is the incumbent has a head start on brand recognition, on

218
00:12:37,260 --> 00:12:41,860
SEO, integrations, customer trust, marketing, all kinds of things.

219
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:45,340
And the fourth is be honest about why you think you can win.

220
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:48,020
All just execute better is not a strategy.

221
00:12:48,020 --> 00:12:51,820
And just copying an existing player is very unlikely to work.

222
00:12:51,820 --> 00:12:55,380
So the questions to ask yourself are, number one, why are customers unhappy with the current

223
00:12:55,380 --> 00:12:56,380
options?

224
00:12:56,380 --> 00:13:00,420
Is it price, features, support, usability, or something else?

225
00:13:00,420 --> 00:13:05,500
Second, is my differentiation something customers actually care about, or is it just interesting

226
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:06,500
to me?

227
00:13:06,500 --> 00:13:11,020
The third is, can I reach customers through a channel the incumbent isn't using well or

228
00:13:11,020 --> 00:13:12,740
where there's some type of opportunity?

229
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:16,740
And the fourth is, is there a specific segment of the market that's underserved and would

230
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:19,300
love a product built just for them?

231
00:13:19,300 --> 00:13:22,740
Method number three is discovering a problem through freelancing.

232
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:26,940
About 10% of the founders we surveyed took this approach.

233
00:13:26,940 --> 00:13:31,980
Examples include Ben Walker, the founder of Drum, which is an ERP for small to medium consulting

234
00:13:31,980 --> 00:13:32,980
firms.

235
00:13:32,980 --> 00:13:36,940
Ben told us, I was consulting as a custom software developer and found that I was building

236
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:39,780
very similar functionality over and over.

237
00:13:39,780 --> 00:13:44,100
I felt that if I could build a very basic version of what I was building my clients,

238
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:48,940
build it as a standalone tool, I should be able to find some early customers and iterate.

239
00:13:48,940 --> 00:13:53,860
John Reynolds, the founder of Civic Review, which is permitting software for local government,

240
00:13:53,860 --> 00:13:58,100
told us, I was a freelance software developer and I was asked to build our product, permitting

241
00:13:58,100 --> 00:13:59,860
software, by municipalities.

242
00:13:59,860 --> 00:14:05,000
I was asked three times and on the third time I decided, okay, these guys can't find a SaaS

243
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,340
solution that fits their needs.

244
00:14:06,340 --> 00:14:11,180
I'll just make my own and sell it so I don't have to keep rewriting the same software.

245
00:14:11,180 --> 00:14:15,580
There's also the chance that you discover a problem to solve that's not something you've

246
00:14:15,580 --> 00:14:20,300
been asked to build multiple times, but rather it's a tool that you need in order to be more

247
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:22,500
efficient in writing your agency.

248
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:27,300
Colin Bartlett, the co-founder of Status Gator, which is a status aggregator, told us, I was

249
00:14:27,300 --> 00:14:31,820
working on a bug ticket for a customer all day and then finally came across the status

250
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:37,260
page for the Facebook ads API and it had a warning about there being intermittent failures.

251
00:14:37,260 --> 00:14:38,780
Turns out that was the bug.

252
00:14:38,780 --> 00:14:43,100
It annoyed me that I did not even know the status page existed, plus the app interfaced

253
00:14:43,100 --> 00:14:45,660
with like 10 other ads APIs.

254
00:14:45,660 --> 00:14:49,580
I thought it would be really nice to have all that on one screen and get an email when

255
00:14:49,580 --> 00:14:50,740
the status changed.

256
00:14:50,740 --> 00:14:52,980
So I built Status Gator for myself.

257
00:14:52,980 --> 00:14:57,220
Note that it's important if you're going to do this, that you think very carefully about

258
00:14:57,220 --> 00:14:58,920
the intellectual property.

259
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:04,700
In a lot of consulting and employment agreements, you will sign away anything you build at the

260
00:15:04,700 --> 00:15:09,780
job and anything you build in your free time, even on your own computers at home.

261
00:15:09,780 --> 00:15:15,180
So you want to be very diligent about this and have all your I's dotted and T's crossed.

262
00:15:15,180 --> 00:15:20,380
Another example is Dump Truck Dispatcher, founded by Joe Walling, no relation.

263
00:15:20,380 --> 00:15:25,100
Dump Truck Dispatcher streamlines the operations and logistics for bulk hauling companies.

264
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:28,460
And Joe told us, at the time I ran a custom software development company.

265
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:30,420
I had a client who wanted this type of software.

266
00:15:30,420 --> 00:15:34,100
I kept the IP and return for charging them less for the software.

267
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:36,800
So let's look at four reasons why this method works.

268
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:41,540
The first is you see the same problems across multiple clients, which validates that demand

269
00:15:41,540 --> 00:15:43,240
exists beyond one company.

270
00:15:43,240 --> 00:15:47,220
The second is that clients are literally paying you to understand their problems.

271
00:15:47,220 --> 00:15:50,480
You're getting paid to do market research and customer development.

272
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:54,920
The third is you build real expertise in the domain as you're getting paid.

273
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:59,180
And the fourth is you can sometimes fund early product development through your agency or

274
00:15:59,180 --> 00:16:00,540
freelancing revenue.

275
00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:04,900
But four things to watch out for include agency work is demanding, finding time to

276
00:16:04,900 --> 00:16:07,460
build a product on the side is genuinely hard.

277
00:16:07,460 --> 00:16:12,620
Number two is make sure IP ownership is clear in your client's contracts before you productized

278
00:16:12,620 --> 00:16:15,480
anything like in the case of Dump Truck Dispatcher.

279
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:20,880
The third is that the transition from freelancing, agency, custom work to actually thinking like

280
00:16:20,880 --> 00:16:23,360
a product owner is harder than it looks.

281
00:16:23,360 --> 00:16:26,260
You have to learn to say no to feature requests.

282
00:16:26,260 --> 00:16:30,360
And the fourth is don't build a product so tailored to one client that no one else wants

283
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:31,360
it.

284
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:34,620
So I have four questions to ask yourself if you're going to take this approach.

285
00:16:34,620 --> 00:16:39,880
The first is what functionality have I built three or more times for different clients?

286
00:16:39,880 --> 00:16:44,500
What internal tools have I built to make my agency more effective or efficient that others

287
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:45,500
might want?

288
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:49,400
Do I have a clear path to transitioning from agency revenue to product revenue?

289
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:51,620
Or will I be stuck doing both forever?

290
00:16:51,620 --> 00:16:56,460
And the last is are my clients in an industry I actually want to serve long-term?

291
00:16:56,460 --> 00:17:00,800
So real quick, if you found any of this helpful so far, can you please give this video a like

292
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:01,960
and subscribe to the channel?

293
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:05,440
My team and I are working really hard to bring you content like this.

294
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:10,840
We put a ton of time and energy into making sure the content is super valuable for you.

295
00:17:10,840 --> 00:17:14,700
And when we see those metrics on YouTube, the likes and the subscribes, it helps us

296
00:17:14,700 --> 00:17:16,760
know that we're on the right track.

297
00:17:16,760 --> 00:17:21,400
So those first three methods all assume you have work experience to draw from.

298
00:17:21,400 --> 00:17:25,840
But what if you're early in your career or you want to look beyond your current industry?

299
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:30,500
Method number four is solving a problem for a spouse, friend, or colleague.

300
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:35,720
And about 8% of respondents said this is how they found their startup idea.

301
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:39,680
In this case, you're going to want to go on the hunt and try to find problems.

302
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:41,980
So you'll want to reach out to your network.

303
00:17:41,980 --> 00:17:45,620
Maybe you talk to your spouse or maybe you talk to your friends to see what problems

304
00:17:45,620 --> 00:17:47,740
they have in their day jobs.

305
00:17:47,980 --> 00:17:53,300
John's sister is the founder of Quill Therapy Solutions, which he describes as software

306
00:17:53,300 --> 00:17:57,860
that generates progress notes and other forms of documentation for mental health therapists

307
00:17:57,860 --> 00:18:02,660
in an ethical and privacy-focused way without recording therapy sessions.

308
00:18:02,660 --> 00:18:04,920
John told us how he came up with the idea.

309
00:18:04,920 --> 00:18:06,800
He said, my wife is a therapist.

310
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:09,380
And she 100% thought of the idea.

311
00:18:09,380 --> 00:18:13,360
She saw me working on a different idea that relied on AI to rearrange information.

312
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:17,080
And she basically said, hey, could you do that for progress notes instead?

313
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:23,240
One way to approach this method is to have a chat with someone who has deep experience.

314
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:28,440
Yoi Sigurdsson is the founder of Crankwheel, which is a screen-sharing, web conferencing,

315
00:18:28,440 --> 00:18:30,520
and video platform for sales.

316
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:35,280
And Yoi told us, so my co-founder, Gilcy, had been working for years in various sales

317
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:40,200
jobs, mostly sort of hybrid telephone and in-person sales, like insurance and banking,

318
00:18:40,200 --> 00:18:41,760
telecoms, that kind of stuff.

319
00:18:41,760 --> 00:18:45,220
And we started brainstorming about what's missing from that kind of space.

320
00:18:45,220 --> 00:18:49,060
We rediscovered a lot of sales enablement tools that already existed.

321
00:18:49,060 --> 00:18:52,660
And I'd been working on real-time communications in Google Chrome at Google.

322
00:18:52,660 --> 00:18:56,560
So I innocently asked him at some point what people use when they're on the phone with

323
00:18:56,560 --> 00:18:59,520
a prospect and they need to show them something.

324
00:18:59,520 --> 00:19:04,080
As it turned out, they didn't use anything because it's too complex for a lot of prospects.

325
00:19:04,080 --> 00:19:08,020
And you don't want to take a good sales call and ruin it with some tech issues.

326
00:19:08,020 --> 00:19:10,420
That's how the idea for Crankwheel was born.

327
00:19:10,420 --> 00:19:13,980
What makes this method work are the following four things.

328
00:19:13,980 --> 00:19:18,280
Number one, you have a direct line to someone experiencing the problem, instant access

329
00:19:18,280 --> 00:19:20,620
for interviews, feedback, and iteration.

330
00:19:20,620 --> 00:19:25,060
Number two, you can deeply understand the problem without having to cold call strangers

331
00:19:25,060 --> 00:19:26,860
at least initially.

332
00:19:26,860 --> 00:19:29,540
Number three, your relationship creates accountability.

333
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:32,880
You're more likely to follow through when someone you care about is counting on you.

334
00:19:32,880 --> 00:19:36,820
And number four, they can introduce you to others in the field for validation and early

335
00:19:36,820 --> 00:19:37,820
sales.

336
00:19:37,820 --> 00:19:39,100
But there are things to watch out for.

337
00:19:39,100 --> 00:19:43,460
The first is your spouse or friend might have a unique problem that others in their field

338
00:19:43,460 --> 00:19:44,460
don't share.

339
00:19:44,460 --> 00:19:46,460
So you want to validate beyond them.

340
00:19:46,460 --> 00:19:50,060
The second one is they might be too nice to tell you your solution isn't good.

341
00:19:50,060 --> 00:19:51,540
So push for honest feedback.

342
00:19:51,540 --> 00:19:55,900
The third is mixing business and personal relationships can get complicated.

343
00:19:55,900 --> 00:19:58,020
So you're going to want to set expectations early.

344
00:19:58,020 --> 00:20:00,520
And finally, you still need to understand the market.

345
00:20:00,520 --> 00:20:04,300
Their expertise plus your building skills isn't enough on its own.

346
00:20:04,300 --> 00:20:08,400
If you're going to attempt this method, you're going to want to ask yourself four questions.

347
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:13,880
The first is, what does my spouse, partner, or close friend complain about in their day

348
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:14,880
job?

349
00:20:14,880 --> 00:20:18,720
The second is, do I think they can introduce me to other people who potentially work in

350
00:20:18,720 --> 00:20:21,120
the same field and have the same problem?

351
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:23,280
It'd be nice to have five intros, for example.

352
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:28,140
The third question is, am I building this to solve a real problem that I think can be

353
00:20:28,140 --> 00:20:29,520
a great business?

354
00:20:29,520 --> 00:20:32,920
Or am I doing it to solve the problem of a person that I care about?

355
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:37,800
And the last one is, do I have the patience to learn an industry that I'm not already

356
00:20:37,800 --> 00:20:39,420
familiar with?

357
00:20:39,420 --> 00:20:44,160
Method number five for finding startup ideas is to find a problem online.

358
00:20:44,160 --> 00:20:48,780
And only 3% of respondents used this approach.

359
00:20:48,780 --> 00:20:54,120
So this is where instead of going to people you know, you are looking for ideas in forums,

360
00:20:54,120 --> 00:20:58,760
in online groups, whether it's Facebook or Reddit or private Slack groups you're in.

361
00:20:58,760 --> 00:21:02,920
Maybe you're lurking in support forums, looking for problems that aren't already solved.

362
00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:07,160
And this method often gets touted online, but it's really hard to do as evidenced by

363
00:21:07,160 --> 00:21:11,200
the fact that only 3% of folks who responded to the survey actually used it.

364
00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:15,320
It didn't show up much in our research, and only a handful of respondents had used this

365
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:16,320
method.

366
00:21:16,320 --> 00:21:19,880
A couple examples are Tolu Akanola, who is the founder of StreamThing.

367
00:21:19,880 --> 00:21:25,000
StreamThing is a Shopify app to enable buyers to select a specific delivery date, for example,

368
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:26,280
for gift purchases.

369
00:21:26,280 --> 00:21:31,120
Tolu told us, someone had recently asked me to help set up a Shopify store, and I noticed

370
00:21:31,120 --> 00:21:34,280
they had an open API, so I decided to build a Shopify app.

371
00:21:34,280 --> 00:21:37,760
I combed through the Shopify support forums to find issues that merchants were complaining

372
00:21:37,760 --> 00:21:38,760
about.

373
00:21:38,760 --> 00:21:42,520
Once I came up with a short list, I looked in their app store to find which apps addressed

374
00:21:42,520 --> 00:21:43,520
the issue.

375
00:21:43,520 --> 00:21:48,800
I prioritized issues that had apps with lots of reviews, but also a lot of negative reviews.

376
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:52,080
This helped identify problems that were pervasive but not well addressed.

377
00:21:52,080 --> 00:21:58,400
Another example is Rami Kufash, the founder of HoverCode, which is a dynamic QR code generator.

378
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:03,240
Dynamic meaning the scan destination can be edited after printing, and you get analytics.

379
00:22:03,240 --> 00:22:08,040
So Rami told us, it was a mixture of having somewhat of a hypothesis or interest in keyword

380
00:22:08,040 --> 00:22:09,040
research.

381
00:22:09,040 --> 00:22:13,480
I found QR codes interesting generally, and after chatting with a friend who works in

382
00:22:13,480 --> 00:22:17,240
marketing, I started to think that marketers were still in the early stages of making use

383
00:22:17,240 --> 00:22:19,600
of QR codes, and there was still opportunity.

384
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:24,040
I did some keyword research with Ahrefs to see if there was demand for QR code tools

385
00:22:24,040 --> 00:22:28,160
and saw huge search volume, which was enough for me to get involved in the space.

386
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:29,560
What makes this work?

387
00:22:29,560 --> 00:22:32,360
Well, there's a bunch of things, but let's cover four.

388
00:22:32,360 --> 00:22:35,400
One is you can discover problems in industries you've never worked in.

389
00:22:35,400 --> 00:22:40,080
Public forums give you unfiltered access to real frustrations and complaints.

390
00:22:40,080 --> 00:22:44,800
You can validate demand through search volume, number of complaints, or size of communities.

391
00:22:44,800 --> 00:22:49,240
And lastly, negative reviews of existing products are a goldmine for differentiation.

392
00:22:49,240 --> 00:22:50,680
But there are things you should watch out for.

393
00:22:50,680 --> 00:22:54,160
The first is people complain about a lot of things they wouldn't actually pay for.

394
00:22:54,160 --> 00:22:56,320
The signal to noise ratio can be low.

395
00:22:56,320 --> 00:22:58,360
You lack the context that insiders have.

396
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:00,960
You might misunderstand the problem or the market dynamics.

397
00:23:00,960 --> 00:23:04,400
The third one is this method takes a lot of time and patience.

398
00:23:04,400 --> 00:23:06,600
Most people give up before finding anything good.

399
00:23:06,600 --> 00:23:11,160
And lastly, selling to people you've never met in an industry you don't understand is

400
00:23:11,160 --> 00:23:12,560
genuinely hard.

401
00:23:12,560 --> 00:23:17,120
Some questions to ask yourself are, am I finding the same complaint repeated by many people,

402
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:18,840
or is this just a one-off gripe?

403
00:23:18,840 --> 00:23:21,760
Would people pay money to solve this, or do they want to vent?

404
00:23:21,760 --> 00:23:25,600
Can I actually reach these people to sell to them, or are they scattered and going to

405
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:27,640
be hard or expensive to find?

406
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:32,040
And am I willing to invest the time to deeply understand an unfamiliar industry before

407
00:23:32,040 --> 00:23:33,320
I start building?

408
00:23:33,320 --> 00:23:37,280
Among our respondents, there were a couple of people who had grown nice marketplace businesses.

409
00:23:37,280 --> 00:23:41,580
And if you're interested in building an add-on for an existing platform, my team has pulled

410
00:23:41,580 --> 00:23:47,280
together a list of over 75 marketplaces at microconf.com slash marketplaces.

411
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:51,920
In addition, you can look at Reddit communities related to specific industries or professions.

412
00:23:51,920 --> 00:23:56,000
You can look at Facebook groups for niche professional communities, support forums for

413
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:00,200
existing products where you want to look for repeated complaints, review sites like

414
00:24:00,200 --> 00:24:06,320
G2, Captera, or App Store reviews, and finally, keyword research tools like Ahrefs, SEMrush,

415
00:24:06,320 --> 00:24:08,560
or even Google's Keyword Planner.

416
00:24:08,560 --> 00:24:13,960
So I have one real-world example from someone who completed the survey, and in this example,

417
00:24:13,960 --> 00:24:17,080
they actually noticed something while they were out in the real world.

418
00:24:17,080 --> 00:24:22,240
So Steven Gladney is the founder of Order Nerd, and Order Nerd aggregates third-party

419
00:24:22,240 --> 00:24:26,920
online ordering apps like DoorDash, Uber Eats, Grubhub into a single platform to make

420
00:24:26,920 --> 00:24:30,320
it easier for restaurant owners and staff to manage.

421
00:24:30,320 --> 00:24:35,180
Steven told us, we already had our team, a sales guy and a developer, and we're actively

422
00:24:35,180 --> 00:24:36,560
hunting ideas.

423
00:24:36,560 --> 00:24:40,360
One day my co-founder was at a restaurant and saw a wall of tablets and began talking to

424
00:24:40,360 --> 00:24:41,360
the staff about it.

425
00:24:41,360 --> 00:24:45,360
The pain in their voice was palpable, I laughed because, man, when you hear a pain point like

426
00:24:45,360 --> 00:24:47,640
that, it's often a good sign.

427
00:24:47,640 --> 00:24:52,240
He texted me a photo and said, it might be the beer talking, but I think this is the

428
00:24:52,240 --> 00:24:53,960
problem we need to solve.

429
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:57,860
Given that I had previously sold to restaurants for five years, and he had over a decade of

430
00:24:57,860 --> 00:25:02,160
experience working with third-party APIs, we knew we were the right people to tackle

431
00:25:02,160 --> 00:25:03,160
this problem.

432
00:25:03,160 --> 00:25:06,920
Obviously, this is a unique example that might be hard to replicate, but it's a good reminder

433
00:25:06,920 --> 00:25:11,760
that by remaining curious and observant, you might find an idea in a place you wouldn't

434
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:12,760
expect.

435
00:25:12,760 --> 00:25:18,720
The number six is to scratch your own itch, and this one was made so popular in the early

436
00:25:18,720 --> 00:25:26,080
and mid-2000s by Basecamp, by DHH and Jason Fried, because they scratched their own itch

437
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:31,240
and it worked for them, and so they would often talk that maybe that's the best way

438
00:25:31,240 --> 00:25:32,400
to start a startup.

439
00:25:32,400 --> 00:25:37,880
And while it's certainly not a bad way, only 16% of our respondents did this.

440
00:25:37,880 --> 00:25:40,620
This is where you build something to solve your own problem, and you discover that other

441
00:25:40,620 --> 00:25:46,440
people have the same issue, and you can find them, and they will pay for it.

442
00:25:46,440 --> 00:25:49,020
So there's a lot that has to come into play.

443
00:25:49,020 --> 00:25:52,580
So there's a lot of things that have to line up in order for this to work.

444
00:25:52,580 --> 00:25:55,060
You can't just say, I have a problem, therefore that's validation.

445
00:25:55,060 --> 00:25:56,060
It's not.

446
00:25:56,060 --> 00:25:59,320
You validated that you will have one customer paying you $0, which is yourself.

447
00:25:59,320 --> 00:26:03,460
So when I think about scratching my own itch, which is, I've done, I built, Drip was an

448
00:26:03,460 --> 00:26:10,260
originally software to scratch your own itch, and I took that idea and went and asked 17

